Figure 1a–d represents a cross-sectional schematic that illustrates, from left to right, the processing that was applied to the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) MXene in order to deplete the surface-terminating species followed by the adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Figure 1a describes an as-prepared single \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) flake terminated by a disordered mixture \((x>2)\), predominantly consisting of O and F.[24,25] Figure 1b shows how an initial thermal treatment of the sheet at \(650~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{h}\) inside the ETEM at a pressure of \(10^{-6}\) mbar results in the desorption of F (see Figure S1 in the Supporting Information), in line with previous investigations.[25] In response to the desorbed F, the remaining O rearranges itself to cover the surface at the thermodynamically preferred fcc-site (Figure 1b).[25,26] With F being depleted and only O remaining, the MXene is locally termination free \((x<2)\). Following this initial thermal procedure, Figure 1c schematically illustrates the subsequent introduction of 8 mbar \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) local gas flow parallel to the sample surface during heating to \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(0.5~\mathrm{h}\), leading to O depletion. Finally, as shown in Figure 1d, 3.5 mbar \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is introduced at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and adsorbed on the depleted surface. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images presented in Figure 1e–h demonstrate the single MXene flake structure throughout the in situ process, with panel (e) showing the as-prepared structure, panel (f) after heating in vacuum, panel (g) after exposure to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), and panel (h) after exposure to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (\(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was chosen to avoid residual hydrocarbon adsorption on the single sheet). Note that the figures, and in particular Figure 1h, are not astigmatic as determined by fast Fourier transform (FFT) (presented in Figure S2 in the Supporting Information); hence, the apparent stripe structure is a result of ordering of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules on the surface.

