During the heating and gas exposure experiments inside the ETEM, the changes in the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) surface chemistry and structure were monitored by electron energy-loss spectroscopy (EELS) and electron diffraction (ED). Residual gas analysis (RGA) was employed to monitor reaction products originating from the interactions of the applied gas and the MXene surface species (shown in Figure S3 in the Supporting Information). Following vacuum heating to remove the F terminations, Figure 2a–c shows the EELS spectra acquired from single flakes after 8 mbar \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) exposure for \(1~\mathrm{h}\) intervals at room temperature (RT), 500, 700, and \(750~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for C–K, Ti–L, and O–K respectively. The shape of the C–K edge in Figure 2a confirms that the chemical environment of C remains constant throughout the experiment. A gradual chemical shift up to \(-1~\mathrm{eV}\) is observed for Ti–L3 and \(\mathrm{Ti}{-}\mathrm{L}_{2}\) above RT that indicates a decrease of O-terminations (caused by reduced charge transfer from Ti to O).[27] The O–K edge is displayed in Figure 2c, and a \(-2\,\mathrm{eV}\) energy shift is observed above RT. A plausible cause of the negative shift of O–K is H saturation of the surface. Most importantly, the integrated intensity of the O–K edge, corresponding to remaining surface terminations, is being reduced with increased temperature. EELS quantification of the Ti:O ratio yields 3:2.1 at RT, 3:1.4 at \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), 3:1.2 at \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and 3:0.6 at \(750~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The chemical shifts of Ti–L and O–K in combination with the loss of O clearly describe a decrease of O-terminations caused by active Ti on the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\) surface. The apparently depleted surface can also be comprehended from Figure 1g, which exhibits a clean appearance. Figure 2d presents the corresponding ED patterns that verify the preservation of the MXene structure up to \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). At \(750~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the ED patterns confirm the formation of TiC nanoplatelets[28] with similar lattice spacing compared to \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\) (see Figure S2 in the Supporting Information). During the MXene exposure to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), RGA detected the formation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) at \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is increasing slightly with temperature (see Figure S3 in the Supporting Information). The formation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) at elevated temperatures correlates well with O-termination depletion which is expected with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) reacting with surface bound O.

The results show that \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) exposure at elevated temperatures is a facile route for removing O-terminations. Consequently, experiments that probe the fundamental MXene properties are attainable. Because of the low pressure (mbar range) of the applied \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gas during the limited time frames, complete
